  "atoms": [
    {
      "id": "a0",
      "pos": 1,
      "text": "Michael Doyle revived the democratic peace thesis in a two-part 1983 article in Philosophy & Public Affairs.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a1",
      "pos": 2,
      "text": "Bruce Russett's 1993 book \"Grasping the Democratic Peace\" tested normative and structural explanations against each other.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a2",
      "pos": 3,
      "text": "Institutional accounts emphasize electoral accountability, legislative checks, and slow military mobilization as constraints on democratic leaders.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a3",
      "pos": 4,
      "text": "Normative accounts emphasize the externalization of domestic habits of peaceful and tolerant conflict resolution.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a4",
      "pos": 5,
      "text": "Democratic leaders face removal from office by voters for costly military failures.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a5",
      "pos": 6,
      "text": "Democratic publics hold a normative aversion to using force against fellow democracies.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a6",
      "pos": 7,
      "text": "Standard conflict datasets record zero interstate wars between two mature democracies.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a7",
      "pos": 8,
      "text": "Standard conflict datasets record at least one interstate war between two mature democracies.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a8",
      "pos": 9,
      "text": "Henry Farber and Joanne Gowa argued that the democratic peace is confined to the post-1945 era and reflects shared Cold War interests.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a9",
      "pos": 10,
      "text": "Erik Gartzke's capitalist peace argument attributes the pattern to economic development, market institutions, and capital openness.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a10",
      "pos": 11,
      "text": "Sebastian Rosato's 2003 article in the American Political Science Review argued that both the institutional and the normative causal mechanisms lack empirical support.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a11",
      "pos": 12,
      "text": "Michael Tomz and Jessica Weeks found in survey experiments that American respondents were less supportive of military strikes against democratic targets.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a12",
      "pos": 13,
      "text": "Rosato concluded that the institutional logic of democratic peace was validated and that only the normative logic failed.",
      "factual": false,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a13",
      "pos": 14,
      "text": "Tomz and Weeks found that respondents were equally willing to endorse strikes against democratic and nondemocratic targets.",
      "factual": false,
      "stance": "asserted"
    }
  ],
