  "response": "Adjudication between the institutional and the normative versions of the democratic peace has always been a quarrel about mechanism rather than about pattern, and the terms of that quarrel were fixed early. Bruce Russett's 1993 book \"Grasping the Democratic Peace\" tested normative and structural explanations against each other, an undertaking brought about by Michael Doyle's revival of the democratic peace thesis in a two-part 1983 article in Philosophy & Public Affairs. Everything since has depended on how the rival mechanisms are specified. Institutional accounts emphasize electoral accountability, legislative checks, and slow military mobilization as constraints on democratic leaders, an emphasis that cannot be squared with the normative accounts, which emphasize the externalization of domestic habits of peaceful and tolerant conflict resolution.\n\nEach mechanism can be restated as a claim about the actors whose conduct it purports to govern. The institutional constraint, that is to say, amounts to the proposition that democratic leaders face removal from office by voters for costly military failures; the normative mechanism, in other words, amounts to the proposition that democratic publics hold a normative aversion to using force against fellow democracies. At least one of the two is at work in democratic dyads, electoral punishment for costly wars or popular distaste for striking fellow democracies, and perhaps both are.\n\nThe observational record is where the two accounts were first expected to part company. Either standard conflict datasets record zero interstate wars between two mature democracies, or they record at least one such war; exactly one of those tallies can be correct and not both, which is why the coding of regime thresholds and battle-death cutoffs has attracted such scrutiny. Even granting the null tally of wars between mature democracies, the inference from it to regime type has been resisted from outside the research program. Henry Farber and Joanne Gowa argued that the democratic peace is confined to the post-1945 era and reflects shared Cold War interests. Erik Gartzke's capitalist peace argument attributes the pattern to economic development, market institutions, and capital openness.\n\nThe mechanism-level literature has produced the sharpest tests of the two logics. Sebastian Rosato's 2003 article in the American Political Science Review argued that both the institutional and the normative causal mechanisms lack empirical support. Michael Tomz and Jessica Weeks found in survey experiments that American respondents were less supportive of military strikes against democratic targets. Rosato concluded that the institutional logic of democratic peace was validated and that only the normative logic failed. That conclusion and the verdict that neither the institutional nor the normative pathway finds empirical support cannot both be true, and the discrepancy has organized much of the subsequent reply literature. Tomz and Weeks found that respondents were equally willing to endorse strikes against democratic and nondemocratic targets, a result that cannot be squared with a reduced public appetite for force against democratic adversaries. The balance therefore now rests on individual-level evidence about accountability and aversion rather than on dyadic war counts alone.",
